{"id": "fd8cd16f-e281-4a5c-b264-cf77e2373aca", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:40:22.889518", "source": "user", "parent_id": "92fbd6a2-b665-4943-990d-00a67c25ee10", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. The inspection is convincing (only task.json exists; offline, no artifacts, no packages) and your design-only framing is correct. Proceed to produce the full experiment design as the final deliverable. Two points from your assumptions I want resolved *within* the design document rather than in another round of questions:\\n\\n1) Split-leakage handling (your item 3/7): commit to a concrete primary framing. My preference: primary endpoint is a leakage-free evaluation on frozen held-out test splits reconstructed with the paper's procedure if the seed is recoverable; otherwise explicitly designate the design as a \\\"fresh-split\\\" comparison and state that claim-strength limitation in a dedicated threats-to-validity section. Also add a secondary diagnostic arm that evaluates on the train splits purely to quantify the optimization-overfit gap (instruction tuned on those items vs. held out). Do not silently choose; make the branch and its consequences explicit.\\n\\n2) Per-task vs global OPRO instruction scope (your item 1/uncertainty 1): since this is unverifiable from materials here, design the condition table so both are representable, mark it as an input-dependent parameter to be filled from the paper appendix/trajectory logs at execution time, and label it clearly as an unresolved source assumption rather than a confirmed fact.\\n\\nAlso keep these from your sketch in the final document: fully crossed condition table with exact prompt templates (byte-level, so empty-prompt and instruction assembly are unambiguous); pinned grader spec per BBH task with a note on substring-match sensitivity; paired-by-item analysis with bootstrap CIs and the pre-registered single omnibus primary contrast plus Holm-corrected per-task secondaries; macro-averaged accuracy as primary metric to mirror the paper; decoding settings (deterministic primary, small temperature>0 robustness arm); the extraction-failure audit log; and a feasibility section stating exactly what external resources (BBH data, model weights/API, grader code) would be required and that nothing was executed here. If any offline dry-run checks are cheap (e.g., grader logic on synthetic items, template-assembly unit checks), you may do them and report results, clearly labeled as design-validation only, not experimental results. Do not report or imply any accuracy numbers as findings; expected-effect statements belong in a hypothesis/power discussion only.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "e9d1aa32-c18a-45a1-94e6-4bcb43833ee0", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:41:27.070882", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "fd8cd16f-e281-4a5c-b264-cf77e2373aca", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Proceeding. First I'll run the cheap, clearly-labeled design-validation checks (byte-level template assembly, grader-logic dry runs on synthetic items, bootstrap/Holm machinery checks), then deliver the full design."}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved and requested a complete design document that incorporates the two resolved points. As needed, they also encouraged performing low-cost offline dry-run checks (grader logic for synthetic items, unit checks for template assembly), and asked to clearly label them as design verification.\n\nLet me do a few low-cost offline verifications:\n1. Template assembly unit check — build prompt templates byte-by-byte in Python, and demonstrate the assembly of an empty prompt vs. an instruction.\n2. Grader logic dry run — implement a mini version of the BBH extra-grade logic (boolean exact match, substring match, date match), run it on synthetic items, and verify that the design's grader spec is implementable.\n\nThese are low-cost and can demonstrate feasibility. After that, create the final stage=result along with the complete design.\n\nLet's write a small Python script that does the following:\n- Define prompt templates and show byte-level outputs for three conditions.\n- Implement simple grading functions (exact match on yes/no, date format check, substring grading via last-line extraction, logical deduction equivalence), and run them on synthetic cases.\n- Bootstrap CI function on synthetic paired differences — this is also design verification (no real data used).\n- Holm correction demo on synthetic p-values.\n\nAll clearly synthetic data. Then end the turn with stage=result and include the complete design document in answer.\n\nKeep the design document comprehensive but within a manageable scope. What to include:\n\n1. Objective and hypotheses (hypothesis/power discussion, no claims of results)\n2. Inputs (dependent on source: OPRO instruction strings — parameter table, per-task vs. global)\n3. Condition table and byte-level prompt templates\n4. Task selection and split protocol (including the committed primary framing branches: seed recovery vs. fresh split; the diagnostic arm for train-split overfitting gap)\n5. Models and decoding settings\n6. Grader spec, with pinned details and notes on substring sensitivity\n7. Metrics and analysis plan (macro-average as primary, omnibus pre-registered contrast, Holm secondary tests, item-level paired bootstrap, temperature > 0 robustness arm, extraction failure audit)\n8. Threats to validity section\n9. Feasibility section (required external resources; nothing executed)\n10. Offline design verification results (label the dry-run checks)\n\nBudget: actor_step_budget 60, question_budget 6 — no questions needed. Let's run the dry-run checks.\n\nCarefully write the dry-run script.\n\nTemplate assembly:\n- Template A (instruction condition): f\"{instruction}\\n\\n{question}\\n\"\n- Empty prompt: exactly question + \"\\n\", with no leading whitespace or newline — to avoid an empty instruction causing a leading \"\\n\\n\".\nUse a sentinel instruction for demonstration. Output repr() to show bytes.\n\nGrader dry run:\n- boolean_expressions: extract \"true\"/\"false\" tokens, exact match.\n- date_understanding: compare normalized dates against a regex.\n- Salient translation / object counting: integer exact match.\n- Navigate: integer.\n- Substring sensitivity demo: grader checks whether the target is contained in the last line; show that a completion \"Answer: yes, probably\" matches \"yes\", but \"not yes\" also matches — indicating sensitivity.\n\nBootstrap demo: synthetic paired data (e.g., per-task mean differences generated from a known normal distribution) → compute bootstrap CI; label as verification.\n\nHolm demo: synthetic p-values [0.01, 0.04, 0.003, ...], apply Holm.\n\nLet's write it all in a single script with clear section markers.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "mkdir -p /workspace/design_validation && cat > /workspace/design_validation/t01_template_assembly.py <<'EOF'\n# DESIGN VALIDATION ONLY - not experimental results.\n# Byte-level prompt-assembly unit checks for the three conditions.\nSENTINEL_Q = \"Q: If all bloops are razzies and all razzies are lazzies, are all bloops definitely lazzies? Yes or no.\\nA:\"\nINSTR_OPRO = \"Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer.\"  # PLACEHOLDER example string; real one filled at execution from paper appendix\nINSTR_COT  = \"Let's think step by step.\"\n\ndef assemble(instruction: str, question: str) -> str:\n    # Canonical assembly rule, pinned by design:\n    # - non-empty instruction: instruction + '\\n\\n' + question\n    # - empty prompt: question verbatim; NO leading separator, no leading/trailing whitespace added\n    if instruction == \"\":\n        return question\n    return instruction + \"\\n\\n\" + question\n\ncases = {\n    \"opro_instruction\": assemble(INSTR_OPRO, SENTINEL_Q),\n    \"cot_baseline\":     assemble(INSTR_COT,  SENTINEL_Q),\n    \"empty_prompt\":     assemble(\"\",          SENTINEL_Q),\n}\nfor name, p in cases.items():\n    print(f\"--- {name} (len={len(p.encode('utf-8'))} bytes) ---\")\n    print(repr(p))\n# Unit checks\nassert assemble(\"\", SENTINEL_Q) == SENTINEL_Q, \"empty-prompt must be question verbatim\"\nassert assemble(INSTR_COT, SENTINEL_Q).startswith(INSTR_COT + \"\\n\\n\"), \"instruction separator must be exactly two newlines\"\nassert \"\\n\\n\\n\" not in assemble(INSTR_OPRO, SENTINEL_Q.rstrip() ) or True\n# guard against accidental double trailing newline when question ends in \\n\nq2 = SENTINEL_Q  # ends with 'A:' no trailing newline here\nassert assemble(\"\", q2).count(\"\\n\\n\") == q2.count(\"\\n\\n\")\nprint(\"TEMPLATE-ASSEMBLY CHECKS: PASS\")\nEOF\npython3 /workspace/design_validation/t01_template_assembly.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-99168926b1417cde", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-99168926b1417cde", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"mkdir -p /workspace/design_validation && cat > /workspace/design_validation/t01_template_assembly.py <<'EOF'\\n# DESIGN VALIDATION ONLY - not experimental results.\\n# Byte-level prompt-assembly unit checks for the three conditions.\\nSENTINEL_Q = \\\"Q: If all bloops are razzies and all razzies are lazzies, are all bloops definitely lazzies? Yes or no.\\\\nA:\\\"\\nINSTR_OPRO = \\\"Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer.\\\"  # PLACEHOLDER example string; real one filled at execution from paper appendix\\nINSTR_COT  = \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"\\n\\ndef assemble(instruction: str, question: str) -> str:\\n    # Canonical assembly rule, pinned by design:\\n    # - non-empty instruction: instruction + '\\\\n\\\\n' + question\\n    # - empty prompt: question verbatim; NO leading separator, no leading/trailing whitespace added\\n    if instruction == \\\"\\\":\\n        return question\\n    return instruction + \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\" + question\\n\\ncases = {\\n    \\\"opro_instruction\\\": assemble(INSTR_OPRO, SENTINEL_Q),\\n    \\\"cot_baseline\\\":     assemble(INSTR_COT,  SENTINEL_Q),\\n    \\\"empty_prompt\\\":     assemble(\\\"\\\",          SENTINEL_Q),\\n}\\nfor name, p in cases.items():\\n    print(f\\\"--- {name} (len={len(p.encode('utf-8'))} bytes) ---\\\")\\n    print(repr(p))\\n# Unit checks\\nassert assemble(\\\"\\\", SENTINEL_Q) == SENTINEL_Q, \\\"empty-prompt must be question verbatim\\\"\\nassert assemble(INSTR_COT, SENTINEL_Q).startswith(INSTR_COT + \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\"), \\\"instruction separator must be exactly two newlines\\\"\\nassert \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\" not in assemble(INSTR_OPRO, SENTINEL_Q.rstrip() ) or True\\n# guard against accidental double trailing newline when question ends in \\\\n\\nq2 = SENTINEL_Q  # ends with 'A:' no trailing newline here\\nassert assemble(\\\"\\\", q2).count(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\") == q2.count(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\")\\nprint(\\\"TEMPLATE-ASSEMBLY CHECKS: PASS\\\")\\nEOF\\npython3 /workspace/design_validation/t01_template_assembly.py\", \"summary\": \"Dry-run byte-level prompt template assembly checks\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-24ab4aff-b450-4887-8e7b-024dc6017085", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Dry-run byte-level prompt template assembly checks", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
{"id": "7d945c30-77b9-4ab6-9b2f-e7fe8ebfaa86", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:41:27.072777", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "e9d1aa32-c18a-45a1-94e6-4bcb43833ee0", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "mkdir -p /workspace/design_validation && cat > /workspace/design_validation/t02_grader_logic.py <<'EOF'\n# DESIGN VALIDATION ONLY - synthetic items, NOT experimental results.\n# Checks that the pinned grader spec (BIG-Bench-Hard extra-grades style) is\n# implementable and demonstrates substring-match sensitivity.\nimport re\n\ndef extract_last_line(completion: str) -> str:\n    lines = [l for l in completion.strip().splitlines() if l.strip()]\n    return lines[-1] if lines else \"\"\n\ndef grade_boolean(completion: str, gold: str) -> int:\n    # BBH boolean_expressions-style: exact token match true/false on extracted answer\n    m = re.findall(r\"\\b(true|false)\\b\", extract_last_line(completion).lower())\n    return int(bool(m) and m[-1] == gold)\n\ndef grade_integer(completion: str, gold: int) -> int:\n    m = re.findall(r\"-?\\d+\", extract_last_line(completion))\n    return int(bool(m) and int(m[-1]) == gold)\n\ndef grade_date(completion: str, gold: str) -> int:\n    # date_understanding-style: normalized (Month DD, YYYY) exact match on extracted line\n    m = re.search(r\"([A-Z][a-z]+)\\s+(\\d{1,2}),\\s*(\\d{4})\", completion)\n    got = f\"{m.group(1)} {int(m.group(2))}, {m.group(3)}\" if m else None\n    return int(got == gold)\n\ndef grade_substring(completion: str, gold: str) -> int:\n    # naive substring grader (what BBH extra-grades improve on) - for sensitivity demo\n    return int(gold.lower() in completion.lower())\n\n# Synthetic cases (task, completion, gold, expected)\nassert grade_boolean(\"blah\\ntrue\", \"true\") == 1\nassert grade_boolean(\"The answer is false.\", \"true\") == 0\nassert grade_integer(\"steps: 7\", 7) == 1\nassert grade_integer(\"not 12, it is 5\", 5) == 1          # last-number heuristic\nassert grade_date(\"It falls on March 3, 2024.\", \"March 3, 2024\") == 1\nassert grade_date(\"June 04, 2009\", \"June 4, 2009\") == 1  # normalization\n# Substring sensitivity demo (design motivation for pinned extra-grades):\nassert grade_substring(\"No, the answer is not yes.\", \"yes\") == 1   # FALSE POSITIVE\nassert grade_substring(\"Answer: Yes\", \"yes\") == 1\nprint(\"GRADER-LOGIC CHECKS: PASS\")\nprint(\"Substring sensitivity demonstrated: 'not yes' matches target 'yes' -> false positive risk,\")\nprint(\"hence last-line extraction + task-specific extra-grades pinned per task.\")\nEOF\npython3 /workspace/design_validation/t02_grader_logic.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bd7bd67e0437f559", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-bd7bd67e0437f559", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"mkdir -p /workspace/design_validation && cat > /workspace/design_validation/t02_grader_logic.py <<'EOF'\\n# DESIGN VALIDATION ONLY - synthetic items, NOT experimental results.\\n# Checks that the pinned grader spec (BIG-Bench-Hard extra-grades style) is\\n# implementable and demonstrates substring-match sensitivity.\\nimport re\\n\\ndef extract_last_line(completion: str) -> str:\\n    lines = [l for l in completion.strip().splitlines() if l.strip()]\\n    return lines[-1] if lines else \\\"\\\"\\n\\ndef grade_boolean(completion: str, gold: str) -> int:\\n    # BBH boolean_expressions-style: exact token match true/false on extracted answer\\n    m = re.findall(r\\\"\\\\b(true|false)\\\\b\\\", extract_last_line(completion).lower())\\n    return int(bool(m) and m[-1] == gold)\\n\\ndef grade_integer(completion: str, gold: int) -> int:\\n    m = re.findall(r\\\"-?\\\\d+\\\", extract_last_line(completion))\\n    return int(bool(m) and int(m[-1]) == gold)\\n\\ndef grade_date(completion: str, gold: str) -> int:\\n    # date_understanding-style: normalized (Month DD, YYYY) exact match on extracted line\\n    m = re.search(r\\\"([A-Z][a-z]+)\\\\s+(\\\\d{1,2}),\\\\s*(\\\\d{4})\\\", completion)\\n    got = f\\\"{m.group(1)} {int(m.group(2))}, {m.group(3)}\\\" if m else None\\n    return int(got == gold)\\n\\ndef grade_substring(completion: str, gold: str) -> int:\\n    # naive substring grader (what BBH extra-grades improve on) - for sensitivity demo\\n    return int(gold.lower() in completion.lower())\\n\\n# Synthetic cases (task, completion, gold, expected)\\nassert grade_boolean(\\\"blah\\\\ntrue\\\", \\\"true\\\") == 1\\nassert grade_boolean(\\\"The answer is false.\\\", \\\"true\\\") == 0\\nassert grade_integer(\\\"steps: 7\\\", 7) == 1\\nassert grade_integer(\\\"not 12, it is 5\\\", 5) == 1          # last-number heuristic\\nassert grade_date(\\\"It falls on March 3, 2024.\\\", \\\"March 3, 2024\\\") == 1\\nassert grade_date(\\\"June 04, 2009\\\", \\\"June 4, 2009\\\") == 1  # normalization\\n# Substring sensitivity demo (design motivation for pinned extra-grades):\\nassert grade_substring(\\\"No, the answer is not yes.\\\", \\\"yes\\\") == 1   # FALSE POSITIVE\\nassert grade_substring(\\\"Answer: Yes\\\", \\\"yes\\\") == 1\\nprint(\\\"GRADER-LOGIC CHECKS: PASS\\\")\\nprint(\\\"Substring sensitivity demonstrated: 'not yes' matches target 'yes' -> false positive risk,\\\")\\nprint(\\\"hence last-line extraction + task-specific extra-grades pinned per task.\\\")\\nEOF\\npython3 /workspace/design_validation/t02_grader_logic.py\", \"summary\": \"Dry-run grader logic checks on synthetic items\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-24ab4aff-b450-4887-8e7b-024dc6017085", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Dry-run grader logic checks on synthetic items", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
